---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ResizePdfPages from '@/components/tools/pdf/ResizePdfPages.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="resize-pdf-pages">
  <ResizePdfPages client:load mode="resize" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Changing the paper size of a PDF</h2>
      <p>
        A PDF page has no fixed &ldquo;size&rdquo; the way a photo has a pixel count. It has a rectangle
        measured in PostScript points (72 to the inch) and drawing instructions placed inside it. To resize
        PDF pages, you decide how big the new rectangle is and what happens to the drawing instructions.
      </p>
      <p>
        This tool lets you set both. You choose the sheet, and you choose whether the content is scaled to
        fit it or kept at its original size and centred. The content stays vector, so nothing is rasterised
        and no quality is lost.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Standard paper sizes in points</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Size</th>
            <th scope="col">Millimetres</th>
            <th scope="col">Inches</th>
            <th scope="col">Points (portrait)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>A3</td><td>297 × 420</td><td>11.7 × 16.5</td><td>841.89 × 1190.55</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A4</td><td>210 × 297</td><td>8.27 × 11.69</td><td>595.28 × 841.89</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A5</td><td>148 × 210</td><td>5.83 × 8.27</td><td>419.53 × 595.28</td></tr>
          <tr><td>US Letter</td><td>216 × 279</td><td>8.5 × 11</td><td>612 × 792</td></tr>
          <tr><td>US Legal</td><td>216 × 356</td><td>8.5 × 14</td><td>612 × 1008</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tabloid</td><td>279 × 432</td><td>11 × 17</td><td>792 × 1224</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        A4 and Letter are the difficult pair. Letter is 6 mm wider and 18 mm shorter, so neither contains
        the other. A4 content scaled onto Letter is at about 94%, with white bands at the top and bottom;
        Letter onto A4 is at about 97%, with bands at the sides. A conversion between the two without bands
        must crop or stretch the content.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Scale to fit versus keep original size</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Scale to fit</strong> multiplies the content by the smaller of the width and height ratios
          and centres it. The aspect ratio stays the same, so nothing is stretched or cut off. This is almost
          always the right choice.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep original size and centre</strong> keeps the content at 100% on the new sheet. Use it to
          shrink the paper around a small drawing, or to put an A5 page on A4 for printing. Content larger
          than the sheet overhangs and is clipped when printed.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The results table shows the exact scale factor for each of the first pages, so you can see whether
        a document was shrunk by 3% or by 30%.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What is lost</h2>
      <p>
        The tool draws each page onto a new sheet, so it rebuilds the page object. Link annotations,
        bookmark destinations, form fields and comment markers belong to the old page object and are not
        copied. Text, fonts, images and vector art are copied.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your document needs its links or form fields and you only need more white space, use
        <a href="/add-margins-to-pdf">Add Margins to PDF</a> in its lossless mode. It enlarges only the page
        rectangle and leaves each object where it was.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Mixed-size documents</h2>
      <p>
        Reports assembled from several sources often mix page sizes, which shows when the printer starts to
        alternate trays. Set the orientation to <strong>match each page</strong> and each page goes on the
        same sheet size in its own orientation. The document then uses one tray and landscape tables stay
        landscape.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
